/* data/learn-c/phy-12-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-05"] = {"5.1":{"s":"Oscillation means moving to and fro about a fixed position: a swing, a clock pendulum, a guitar string, a tuning fork. This chapter builds the maths of the simplest kind, simple harmonic motion, and then looks at pendulums, damping and resonance."},"5.2":{"s":"Periodic motion repeats itself after equal intervals of time. Oscillation is periodic motion back and forth about a mean position. Every oscillation is periodic, but not every periodic motion (like the Earth going round the Sun) is an oscillation.","w":["Every oscillation is periodic, but a periodic motion (a planet's orbit) need not be an oscillation."]},"5.3":{"s":"Linear S.H.M.: the restoring force always points to the mean position and is proportional to the displacement, f = −kx. The constant k is the force constant. The minus sign means force and displacement are in opposite directions. A block on a spring on a smooth table is the standard example.","f":[{"x":"f = -k*x","n":"Restoring force in linear S.H.M.; k = force constant, x = displacement from the mean position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.3, p.110"},{"x":"a = -(k/m)*x","n":"Acceleration: proportional to the displacement and towards the mean position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.3, p.110"}],"w":["The minus sign is the whole point: force and displacement are always opposite."]},"5.4":{"s":"Putting f = −kx into Newton's law gives the differential equation of S.H.M.: d²x/dt² + ω²x = 0, where ω² = k/m. Any motion that obeys this equation is simple harmonic. ω is called the angular frequency.","f":[{"x":"d^2x/dt^2 + omega^2*x = 0","n":"Differential equation of linear S.H.M.; omega^2 = k/m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.4, p.112"}]},"5.5":{"s":"Solving the equation gives x = A sin(ωt + α). Velocity is v = ω√(A² − x²): largest at the mean position and zero at the extremes. Acceleration is a = −ω²x: zero at the mean position and largest at the extremes. A is the amplitude.","f":[{"x":"x = A*sin(omega*t + phi)","n":"Displacement; A = amplitude, phi = initial phase. Starting from an extreme, x = A cos(omega t).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.5, p.113"},{"x":"v = omega*sqrt(A^2 - x^2)","n":"Speed at displacement x; v_max = A*omega at the mean position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.5, p.112"},{"x":"a = -omega^2*x","n":"Acceleration; largest, omega^2 A, at the extremes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.5, p.112"}],"w":["At the mean position speed is largest and acceleration zero; at the extremes it is the other way round."]},"5.6":{"s":"The amplitude A is the largest displacement from the mean position. The period T is the time for one full oscillation, and the frequency n = 1/T is the number of oscillations per second. For a mass on a spring, a heavier mass gives a longer period and a stiffer spring a shorter one, whatever the amplitude. Springs joined end to end make a softer combination; springs side by side make a stiffer one.","f":[{"x":"T = 2*pi/omega","n":"Period of S.H.M.; omega = angular frequency, n = 1/T = omega/(2*pi).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.115"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(m/k)","n":"Mass m on a spring of force constant k.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.115"},{"x":"n = (1/(2*pi))*sqrt(k/m)","n":"Frequency of the same system.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.115"},{"x":"1/k_s = 1/k_1 + 1/k_2","n":"Two springs in series: effective force constant k_s (less than either).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.116"},{"x":"k_p = k_1 + k_2","n":"Two springs in parallel: effective force constant k_p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.6, p.116"}],"w":["The period depends on mass and force constant, not on the amplitude.","Series springs: the combined k is smaller than each. Parallel springs: the k's add."]},"5.7":{"s":"Reference circle: if a point moves uniformly on a circle, its shadow (projection) on a diameter performs S.H.M. The circle's radius is the amplitude and its angular speed is ω. This gives an easy way to picture and derive the S.H.M. equations.","w":["The shadow of uniform circular motion on ANY diameter is S.H.M.; the circle's radius is the amplitude."]},"5.8":{"s":"Phase tells the state of oscillation at an instant: where the particle is, which way it is moving, and in which oscillation. Position alone is not enough, because the particle passes the same point moving both ways. Phase is written as the angle (ωt + α); α is the initial phase (epoch).","w":["Position alone does not fix the phase: the particle passes each point twice per cycle, moving opposite ways."]},"5.9":{"s":"S.H.M. can be drawn as graphs against time. Displacement, velocity and acceleration are all sine or cosine curves. Velocity is a quarter cycle (π/2) ahead of displacement, and acceleration is half a cycle (π) away from displacement.","w":["Velocity is π/2 ahead of displacement; acceleration is π away from displacement."]},"5.10":{"s":"Two S.H.M.s with the same period along the same line add up to another S.H.M. of the same period. Its amplitude depends on the phase difference: largest (A₁ + A₂) when they are in phase and smallest (the difference) when they are opposite.","f":[{"x":"R = sqrt(A_1^2 + A_2^2 + 2*A_1*A_2*cos(phi_1 - phi_2))","n":"Amplitude of the sum of two S.H.M.s of the same period along one line.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.10, p.120"},{"x":"tan(delta) = (A_1*sin(phi_1) + A_2*sin(phi_2))/(A_1*cos(phi_1) + A_2*cos(phi_2))","n":"Initial phase of the resultant S.H.M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.10, p.120"}],"w":["In phase: R = A₁ + A₂. Opposite phase: R = |A₁ − A₂|. At 90°: R = √(A₁² + A₂²)."]},"5.11":{"s":"A particle in S.H.M. has kinetic energy because it moves and potential energy because of the restoring force. The kinetic energy is greatest at the mean position and zero at the extremes; the potential energy is the other way round. Their sum is constant, equal to ½mω²A², so it depends on the square of the amplitude. The two energies are equal at x = ±A/√2.","f":[{"x":"E_k = (1/2)*m*omega^2*(A^2 - x^2)","n":"Kinetic energy at displacement x.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"},{"x":"E_p = (1/2)*m*omega^2*x^2","n":"Potential energy at displacement x (= (1/2) k x^2).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"},{"x":"E = (1/2)*m*omega^2*A^2 = (1/2)*k*A^2","n":"Total energy: constant, the same at every position.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"},{"x":"E = 2*pi^2*n^2*A^2*m","n":"Total energy in terms of the frequency n.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.11, p.121"}],"w":["Doubling the amplitude makes the total energy four times larger.","At the mean position all the energy is kinetic; at the extremes all of it is potential."]},"5.12":{"s":"A simple pendulum is a small heavy bob on a light, inextensible string. For small swings its motion is S.H.M. with period T = 2π√(l/g). The period depends on length and g, not on the mass or (for small swings) the amplitude. A seconds pendulum has a period of 2 s.","f":[{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g)","n":"Simple pendulum, small swings; L = length to the centre of the bob.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.12, p.123"},{"x":"L_s = g/pi^2","n":"Length of a seconds pendulum (period 2 s), about 0.99 m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.12, p.123"}],"w":["The period does not depend on the bob's mass, or on the amplitude while it stays small.","A seconds pendulum has a period of 2 s, not 1 s: each one-way swing takes 1 s."]},"5.13":{"s":"Angular S.H.M. is oscillation in which the restoring torque is proportional to the angular displacement and opposite to it. Examples are a disc hung on a twisted wire and a magnet turned slightly in a magnetic field. The torque −cθ gives an angular acceleration proportional to −θ, so the angle oscillates in S.H.M. The period depends on the moment of inertia and the torsion constant.","f":[{"x":"tau = -c*theta","n":"Restoring torque on a twisted wire; c = torque per unit angular displacement.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.125"},{"x":"I*d^2(theta)/dt^2 + c*theta = 0","n":"Differential equation of angular S.H.M.; I = moment of inertia.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.125"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(I/c)","n":"Period of angular S.H.M., from T = 2 pi over the square root of the angular acceleration per unit angular displacement.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.125"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(I/(mu*B))","n":"Small oscillations of a magnet of moment mu in a field B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.13, p.126"}],"w":["It is S.H.M. only for small angles, where sin θ ≈ θ.","Two magnets joined with like poles together add their moments; with unlike poles together the moments subtract."]},"5.14":{"s":"In a real oscillator, friction or air resistance removes energy, so the amplitude slowly gets smaller. This is damped oscillation. The amplitude falls off exponentially with time.","f":[{"x":"x = A*e^(-b*t/(2*m))*cos(omega'*t + phi)","n":"Damped oscillation; b = damping constant (damping force = -b v).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.14, p.127"},{"x":"omega' = sqrt(k/m - (b/(2*m))^2)","n":"Angular frequency with damping: less than without.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.14, p.127"}],"w":["Damping lowers the frequency a little (longer period) and makes the amplitude fall exponentially."]},"5.15":{"s":"Free oscillations happen at the body's own natural frequency. Forced oscillations happen when an outside periodic force drives the body at the driver's frequency. When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, the amplitude becomes very large. This is resonance, as when soldiers break step on a bridge or a radio is tuned to a station.","f":[{"x":"n = (1/(2*pi))*sqrt(g/l)","n":"Natural frequency of a simple pendulum: it oscillates freely at this frequency.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 5.15, p.127"}],"w":["Resonance needs the driving frequency to equal the natural frequency, not just a large driving force."]}};
